  "apply": [
    "Each row is [name, ordered replacements, [criterion label, reason label], additional assertions]. Start from independent defaults. Apply JSON Pointer replacements in order; an index equal to array length appends. All ids, models, source references and evidence are synthetic. Acceptance labels are fixture assertions, not canonical Runtime statuses.",
    "Resolve @sha:TEXT as SHA-256 of literal UTF-8 TEXT and @jsonsha:TEXT as SHA-256 of compact ASCII JSON encoding of the literal string (including quotes), matching #204 correlation. Resolve @rubric:KEY as SHA-256 of compact UTF-8 JSON of rubrics[KEY], with object keys sorted recursively; each digest is also pinned in rubric_sha256. Resolve @record:FIELD after applying changes, using raw UTF-8 bytes for output_utf8 and tool_return_utf8 and recursively sorted compact JSON for structured values.",
    "The default evaluator subject is the exact original task, declared in rubric-r1. Source versions and authority come from the pinned defaults and their synthetic evidence policy. A changed model, result, recipient or expectation never supplies its own authorization. Proposal reason/label spelling may be explicitly mapped from an implementation's typed evidence.",
    "Absent usage observations expand to unknown per field, never to zero. V1 allowed, refused or held and completed transport remain separate from typed API/Tool results and effect. Where this fixture calls for an effect, the trusted postcondition proof is a separate input. A reviewer result cannot resolve deterministic failure or unverified contradictory evidence.",
    "Record the affected criterion/revision, applicability, safe correlation, expected/observed values and reason, origin/access status and evidence references. Assign an owner and next check for material gaps. Handoff proposals and no-effect assertions describe this evaluator invocation, not the synthetic observations. A declared rubric change creates a separately linked reevaluation; the old record stays pinned. Human decision is absent in every fixture."
  ],
